{"authored" [{"question" "q-15657" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-3,\\,\\,\\,y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039508/state.json" "file_sha256" "12fffa75881fdf84fcaa4c6090605923e526bf36a9fed64cd4ce6c8c12ac5a01" "basis" 1365 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039508/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-15657" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-3\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=x+3 \\\\ y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Pythagorean identity ${sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t=1$, we get\n\n$$\n(x+3)^{2}+{y}^{2}=1.\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $(x+3)^{2}+{y}^{2}=1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039508/state.json" "file_sha256" "12fffa75881fdf84fcaa4c6090605923e526bf36a9fed64cd4ce6c8c12ac5a01" "basis" 1365 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039508/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-15657" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039508/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a6ff0a51ac75a21025e9d0e9a22eb512febb63f2fd44629e2b5f9555cf2074bb" "basis" 1365 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differen