040368/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f39e7304fc8786cd410dfffae649df7478b12d9373868e57d0bed4901a3c17a"} {"question" "q-15657" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-3\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=x+3 \\\\ y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Pythagorean identity ${sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t=1$, we get\n\n$$\n(x+3)^{2}+{y}^{2}=1.\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $(x+3)^{2}+{y}^{2}=1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040368/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f39e7304fc8786cd410dfffae649df7478b12d9373868e57d0bed4901a3c17a"} {"question" "q-15657" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040368/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a6400a7a545edbfdebc704f49a453432385030e1be732b38d94042e8eb159cef"} {"question" "q-15657" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040368/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f39e7304fc8786cd410dfffae649df7478b12d9373868e57d0bed4901a3c17a"} {"question" "q-15657" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(x+3)^{2}+{y}^{2}=1"] ["math" "(x-3)^{2}+(y-3)^{2}=3"] ["math" "3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1"] ["math" "{x}^{2}+(y-3)^{2}=1"] ["math" "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=3"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040368/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f39e7304fc8786cd410dfffae649df7478b12d9373868e57d0bed4901a3c17a"} {"question" "q-15657" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(x+3)^{2}+{y}^{2}=1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040368/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f39e7304fc8786cd410dfffae649df7478b12d9373868e57d0bed4901a3c17a"} {"question" "q-24575" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\,\\,y=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040368/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f39e7304fc8786cd410dfffae649df7478b12d9373868e57d0bed4901a3c17a"} {"question" "q-24575" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=-\\frac{x}{3} \\\\ y=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=\\frac{y}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using double angle formula for cosine and the Pythagorean identity, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t & ={cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡t-(1-{cos}^{2}⁡t) \\\\  & =2{cos}^{2}⁡t-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=-\\frac{x}{3}$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=\\frac{y}{4}$ into this formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{4} & =2{(-\\frac{x}{3})}^{2}-1 \\\\ \\frac{y}{4} & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{9}-1 \\\\ y & =4(\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{9}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=4(\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{9}-1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040368/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f39e7304fc8786cd410dfffae649df7478b12d9373868e57d0bed4901a3c17a"} {"question" "q-24575" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040368/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a6400a7a545edbfdebc704f49a453432385030e1be732b38d94042e8eb159cef"} {"question" "q-24575" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040368/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f39e7304fc8786cd410dfffae649df7478b12d9373868e57d0bed4901a3c17a"} {"question" "q-24575" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=1+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{6}"] ["math" "y=1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}"] ["math" "y=4(\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{9}-1)"] ["math" "y=7(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+1)"] ["math" "y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}-1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040368/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f39e7304fc8786cd410dfffae649df7478b12d9373868e57d0bed4901a3c17a"} {"question" "q-24575" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=4(\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{9}-1)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040368/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f39e7304fc8786cd410dfffae649df7478b12d9373868e57d0bed4901a3c17a"} {"question" "q-102664" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically by\n\n$$\nx=1-2t,\\,\\,\\,y=(t-2)^{2}(t+1),\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040368/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f39e7304fc8786cd410dfffae649df7478b12d9373868e57d0bed4901a3c17a"} {"question" "q-102664" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First, we use the $x$ equation to solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=1-2t\\,⇒\\,t=\\frac{1-x}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $t=\\frac{1-x}{2}$ into our expression for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={(\\frac{1-x}{2}-2)}^{2}(\\frac{1-x}{2}+1) \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1-x-4}{2})}^{2}(\\frac{1-x+2}{2}) \\\\  & ={(\\frac{-x-3}{2})}^{2}(\\frac{3-x}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}(x+3)^{2}(x-3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=-\\frac{1}{8}(x+3)^{2}(x-3).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040368/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f39e7304fc8786cd410dfffae649df7478b12d9373868e57d0bed4901a3c17a"} {"question" "q-102664" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040368/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a6400a7a545edbfdebc704f49a453432385030e1be732b38d94042e8eb159cef"} {"question" "q-102664" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040368/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f39e7304fc8786cd410dfffae649df7478b12d9373868e57d0bed4901a3c17a"} {"question" "q-102664" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=-\\frac{1}{2}(x+3)^{2}(x-3)"] ["math" "y=-\\frac{1}{4}(x+3)(x-3)^{2}"] ["math" "y=-\\frac{1}{8}(x+3)^{2}(x-3)"] ["math" "y=\\frac{1}{4}(x+3)^{2}(x-3)"] ["math" "y=\\frac{1}{8}(x+3)(x-3)^{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040368/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f39e7304fc8786cd410dfffae649df7478b12d9373868e57d0bed4901a3c17a"} {"question" "q-102664" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=-\\frac{1}{8}(x+3)^{2}(x-3)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040368/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f39e7304fc8786cd410dfffae649df7478b12d9373868e57d0bed4901a3c17a"} {"question" "q-320926" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{t+3},\\,\\,\\,y=\\frac{t}{-2t+1},\\,t\\ne -3,\\,\\,\\,t\\ne \\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this curve is\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,x\\ne $\n{{field-2}}$,\\,\\,\\,x\\ne 0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040368/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f39e7304fc8786cd410dfffae649df7478b12d9373868e57d0bed4901a3c17a"} {"question" "q-320926" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First, we use the $x$ equation to solve for $t:$\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{t+3}\\,⇒\\,t=-3+\\frac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $t=-3+\\frac{1}{x}$ into our expression for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{(-3+\\frac{1}{x})}{(-2\\,(-3+\\frac{1}{x})+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-3+\\frac{1}{x})}{(6-\\frac{2}{x}+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-3+\\frac{1}{x})}{(7-\\frac{2}{x})} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3x+1}{\\cancel{x}}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{x}}{7x-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-3x}{7x-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=\\frac{1-3x}{7x-2}.$\n\nFinally, since $t\\ne \\frac{1}{2}$ and $x=\\frac{1}{t+3}$ has range $x\\ne 0,$ we must have \n$x\\ne \\frac{2}{7},\\text{ }0.$ \n\nTherefore, the complete Cartesian equation is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1-3x}{7x-2},\\,x\\ne \\frac{2}{7},\\,\\,\\,x\\ne 0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040368/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f39e7304fc8786cd410dfffae649df7478b12d9373868e57d0bed4901a3c17a"} {"question" "q-320926" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040368/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a6400a7a545edbfdebc704f49a453432385030e1be732b38d94042e8eb159cef"} {"question" "q-320926" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040368/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f39e7304fc8786cd410dfffae649df7478b12d9373868e57d0bed4901a3c17a"} {"question" "q-320926" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1-3x}{7x-2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040368/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f39e7304fc8786cd410dfffae649df7478b12d9373868e57d0bed4901a3c17a"} {"question" "q-320926" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040368/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f39e7304fc8786cd410dfffae649df7478b12d9373868e57d0bed4901a3c17a"} {"question" "q-320926" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{2}{7}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040368/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f39e7304fc8786cd410dfffae649df7478b12d9373868e57d0bed4901a3c17a"} {"question" "q-320946" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\,\\,y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this curve is\n\n{{field-1}}$=1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040368/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f39e7304fc8786cd410dfffae649df7478b12d9373868e57d0bed4901a3c17a"} {"question" "q-320946" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=\\frac{x}{3} \\\\ y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=y+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Pythagorean identity ${sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t=1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{x}{3})}^{2}+(y+2)^{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{9}{x}^{2}+(y+2)^{2} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040368/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f39e7304fc8786cd410dfffae649df7478b12d9373868e57d0bed4901a3c17a"} {"question" "q-320946" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040368/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a6400a7a545edbfdebc704f49a453432385030e1be732b38d94042e8eb159cef"} {"question" "q-320946" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040368/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f39e7304fc8786cd410dfffae649df7478b12d9373868e57d0bed4901a3c17a"} {"question" "q-320946" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{x^2}{9}+(y+2)^2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040368/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f39e7304fc8786cd410dfffae649df7478b12d9373868e57d0bed4901a3c17a"}] "usage" {"q-15657" []} "basis" 1430 "retractions" [] "immutable_answers" [17592186302855 17592186302856 17592186302857 17592186302858 17592186302859] "immutable_fields" [[17592186302854 :answer-field/correct] [17592186302854 :answer-field/id] [17592186302854 :answer-field/key] [17592186302854 :answer-field/type]] "no_histor